.TH BTFS 4
.SH NAME
btfs, btbench \- Bluetooth Low Energy as a file system
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bt/btfs
[
.B -u
] [
.BI -m " mountpoint"
] [
.BI -k " keyfile"
] [
.I controller
]
.PP
.B bt/btbench
[
.I controller
[
.I mountpoint
]
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Btfs
is a Bluetooth Low Energy stack.  All of it above the controller is
an ordinary user program: the kernel carries HCI packets and does
nothing else, which it already knows how to do \- a byte stream
through
.IR devuart (3)
for a UART-attached controller, endpoints through
.IR devusb (3)
for a USB one.
.PP
Bluetooth's own structure is a hierarchy \- a controller, the devices
it can hear, the services each device has, the characteristics inside
a service \- so
.I btfs
serves it as one, and that name space is the whole interface.  There
are no other calls: a program uses Bluetooth here by opening, reading
and writing files.
.PP
.I Controller
names the transport.  For the default H4 transport it is a byte
stream and, optionally, its speed and whether to use RTS/CTS flow
control:
.IP
.EX
bt/btfs /dev/eia1!115200!fc
.EE
.PP
It may instead be a
.IR dial (2)
string, which is how a machine whose only serial port is its console
reaches a controller elsewhere; the framing is the same either way.
With
.B -u
the transport is USB instead, and
.I controller
is the device directory under
.B /dev/usb
.RB ( ep3.0 ,
say), or empty to take the first Bluetooth controller found there.
.PP
.B -m
gives the mount point, default
.BR /net/bt .
.B -k
names a file the bonding keys are read from at start and written back
to as they are made; without it they last only as long as
.I btfs
does.
.SS The name space
.TF /net/bt/0/remote/ADDR/gatt
.PD
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/addr
The controller's own address.
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/version
What the controller says it is.
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/state
One line: the address, whether it is scanning, how many devices have
been heard, how many connections are open, and the controller's ACL
packet size and buffer count.
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/scan
.B on
or
.BR off .
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/bonds
One line per bond: the peer, its address type, and the keys.
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/ctl
Writing
.B "scan on"
or
.B "scan off"
starts and stops scanning.
.B "connect \fIaddr\fP [public|random]",
.BI "disconnect " addr
and
.BI "pair " addr
act on one device.
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/remote/\fIADDR\fP/
A device that has been heard from, named by its address written most
significant byte first \- or, once a bond gives it one, by its
identity address, so a device that rotates a resolvable private
address keeps one directory rather than gaining one every few minutes.
It holds
.BR addr ,
which is the address it is answering on now and changes under a
rotating device,
.B identity
(the identity address behind it and the address it was resolved from,
or
.B none
for a device that has not distributed an identity key),
.B type
(public or random),
.B rssi
and
.B name
from its advertisement,
.B adv
(the whole advertisement in hexadecimal),
.BR state ,
and a
.B ctl
that takes
.BR connect ,
.B disconnect
and
.B pair
without an address.
.TP
.B /net/bt/0/remote/\fIADDR\fP/gatt/
Appears when the device is connected: a directory per service, named
by its UUID, holding a file per characteristic, named by its UUID.
Reading one reads the characteristic off the device; writing one
writes it.
.PD
.PP
A characteristic that notifies or indicates also has a
.IB uuid .notify
beside it.  Opening that file subscribes to the characteristic, each
read returns the next notification whole, and closing it
unsubscribes.
.PP
So a battery level is
.IP
.EX
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/gatt/180f/2a19
.EE
.PP
and watching it change is
.IP
.EX
cat /net/bt/0/remote/345678ABCDEF/gatt/180f/2a19.notify
.EE
.SS Btbench
.I Btbench
starts
.I btfs
on the transport it is given and then drives the whole stack through
its files, printing what happens at each step: the controller, a
scan, what answered, a connection, the remote device's whole GATT
database, a characteristic read, three notifications, pairing, and a
disconnection.  It is the test program, and it runs unchanged under
.IR emu (1)
and inside the native kernels.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/bt
.br
.B /appl/lib/bt
.br
.B /module/bt.m
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR bt (2),
.IR dial (2),
.IR devuart (3),
.IR devusb (3),
.IR usbd (8)
.SH BUGS
.PP
This is Bluetooth Low Energy only.  There is no Bluetooth Classic:
no inquiry, no SDP, no RFCOMM, no HID, no PAN, and no audio.  L2CAP
here is the fixed channels an LE link uses \- ATT, LE signalling and
security management \- and the length and CID framing under them;
there are no connection-oriented channels.
.PP
Pairing is LE legacy pairing with a zero temporary key, which is Just
Works: it establishes an encrypted link and a bond, and it gives no
protection at all against a man in the middle.  There is no passkey
entry and no numeric comparison.  LE Secure Connections is not
implemented \- it needs P-256 elliptic curve Diffie-Hellman, which is
not here \- so a device that insists on it will not pair, and says so.
.PP
The USB transport
.RB ( -u )
is written and compiles but has never moved a packet.  There is no
USB Bluetooth controller on the bench and no emulated one, so nothing
about it is demonstrated; only the H4 transport is.
.PP
On
.B pc64
the H4 transport over the second serial port makes the kernel fault:
sustained traffic on that uart ends in a general protection violation
at the
.B IRETQ
in
.B _intrr
.RB ( os/inferno/pc64/l.s ),
with a corrupt interrupt frame.  That is a fault in the kernel's own
interrupt return, not in this stack: the same Dis code over a
.IR dial (2)
transport completes on the same
.B pc64
kernel and on
.BR riscv64 .
See
.B doc/inferno-ports.ms.
.PP
Only the initiating (central) side is implemented.  This stack does
not advertise and cannot be connected to.
.PP
A resolvable private address is resolved against every stored bond's
identity resolving key, so a device that rotates its address \- which
most modern peripherals do \- stays one device under one directory,
named by its identity, and the bond made with it remains reachable.
Resolution is done here rather than by handing the keys to the
controller's resolving list.
.PP
The name a directory is given when the device is first heard from is
kept.  So a device first seen from a private address, and paired only
afterwards, keeps the directory named by that first private address
even though its later addresses resolve to it: the name is settled
before there is a key to resolve it with, and a directory a client may
be holding open is not renamed underneath it.  Its
.B identity
file says what it really is.
.PP
A bond read from the
.B -k
key file at startup resolves addresses just as one made in the session
does, so a device bonded with earlier is recognised again without
pairing.
.PP
Pairing is still LE legacy Just Works, so the identity key is
distributed without protection against a man in the middle, and
resolution is only as trustworthy as the pairing that produced the
key.
.PP
.B scripts/btbench.sh
runs
.I btbench
under
.I emu
against the controller model in
.B scripts/bthci.py ,
in two phases -- pairing, then meeting the bond again from a key file
\- and exits non-zero if either fails.
.PP
.B doc/bluetooth-plan.ms
lists what is missing in the order the pieces block each other, and
plans the 9front implementation.
